      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "In the given problem, we are asked to find the maximum possible length of $\\overline{OB}$ when two rays with a $30^\\circ$ angle at point $O$ have points $A$ and $B$ on them such that $AB=1$.\n\nTo solve this problem, consider rotating point $A$ counterclockwise around $O$ by $30^\\circ$ (since the initial angle between the rays is $30^\\circ$). After this rotation, point $A$ maps to point $B$. This is because both rays had an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the line $OB$.\n\nWe now need to determine the maximum length of $\\overline{OB}$ under the constraint that $AB = 1$. Let $x$ be the distance from $O$ to point $B$ as measured onto $OB$ (this will be the length of $\\overline{OB}$), and let $y$ be the (negative of the) distance from $B$ to point $A$ (since we are rotating $A$ counterclockwise by $30^\\circ$ to reach $B$). Thus, we have the following relationships:\n\n\\[ y = AB = 1 \\]\n\\[ x \\sin(30^\\circ) = -1 \\]\n\\[ x \\cos(30^\\circ) = 0 \\]\n\nFrom the equation involving the sine term, we get:\n\n\\[ x \\sin(30^\\circ) = x \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = -1 \\]\n\\[ x = -2 \\]\n\nHowever, since $x$ represents the distance, and distances are positive values, we should consider the absolute value of the result:\n\n\\[ x = 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible length of $\\overline{OB}$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
