      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The maximum value occurs when points $A$ and $B$ are directly aligned to one another on the line through $O$. Using the Law of Cosines, we can find the length of $\\overline{OB}$ as $1 + \\cos(30^\\circ) \\approx \\boxed{\\sqrt{3} + 1}$.\nThe given problem involves two rays with a $30^\\circ$ angle between them and a length of $1$ for the line segment $\\overline{AB}$.\nTo find the maximum possible length of $\\overline{OB}$, we draw a right triangle $\\triangle AOB$ with $\\overline{OB}$ as the hypotenuse and $\\overline{AB}$ as one of the legs.\nBy the Pythagorean theorem, we have:\n\\[ \\overline{OB}^2 = \\overline{AB}^2 + \\overline{AO}^2 \\]\n\\[ \\overline{OB}^2 = 1^2 + \\overline{AO}^2 \\]\n\\[ \\overline{OB}^2 = 1 + \\overline{AO}^2 \\]\nSince $\\overline{AO}$ is the remaining length of the other ray in the $30^\\circ$ angle, we have $\\overline{AO} = \\overline{OB} \\cos(30^\\circ) = \\frac{\\overline{OB}}{2} \\sqrt{3}$.\nSubstituting this into the equation for $\\overline{OB}^2$, we get:\n\\[ \\overline{OB}^2 = 1 + \\left( \\frac{\\overline{OB}}{2} \\sqrt{3} \\right)^2 \\]\n\\[ \\overline{OB}^2 = 1 + \\frac{3\\overline{OB}^2}{4} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\overline{OB}^2 = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\\[ \\overline{OB}^2 = 1 \\]\nTaking the positive square root (since length cannot be negative), we find that:\n\\[ \\overline{OB} = \\sqrt{1} \\]\n\\[ \\overline{OB} = \\boxed{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\]",
